jaffle shop corpus — what the data is, and the license that ships with it
=========================================================================

What the data is
----------------
The Jaffle Shop corpus this directory ships is derived from a run made by the maintainers
of this repository, once, on 2026-09-24, by
running jafgen 0.4.14 (the jaffle shop generator, github.com/dbt-labs/jaffle-shop-generator,
tag v0.4.14) as `jafgen 1 --pre raw`. The run's first calendar quarter was then cut out by
derive/trim.py, and the later steps of the derivation chain in PROVENANCE move it in time
and add addresses. PROVENANCE records the exact run, the trim rule and the chain. Apart
from those later steps, every row is a row of that run's output, byte for byte; the trim
only leaves rows out. They are our own output: they were not copied from any dataset
published by dbt Labs or anyone else, and they are not regenerated by anything in this
repository.

The rows are synthetic. Identifiers, timestamps, order contents and amounts come from
the generator's random simulation. What the simulation draws from is fixed text in
jafgen's own source code: the store city names and tax rates, the product and supply
names, descriptions, prices and costs, and the sentences the tweets are assembled from.
Customer names were drawn by Faker 40.39.0, a library jafgen depends on. None of it
describes a real person, order or store. One file, raw_supplies.csv, is written from
that fixed text alone, with nothing random in it, and the trim keeps it whole, so it is
byte-identical to the file of the same name in dbt-labs/jaffle-shop-data: the same
generator wrote the same text. It was generated here, not copied from there.

